CHAPTER EIGHTEEN: THE EPILOGUE (1987)
Five years later, Mercy Station was reopened.
The official reason was that structural repairs had been completed and the facility was now safe for regular operations. The unofficial reason was that the station had become useful again.
Arun Dey, now a high-ranking Deputy Director of the Railway Board, stood on the platform. He was immaculately dressed, efficient, and cold. The weight he'd carried for five years—the weight of what he'd become—had calcified into something that felt almost like normalcy.
The Lucknow express pulled in at 7:15 PM. The timing was perfect. The compartment geometry was correct. Everything was aligned.
A young journalist descended from Compartment Three.
There was a crowd, a sudden commotion, and a fall.
The body was found near the water tank. But this time, there were no physical anomalies. There were no post-mortem fractures where investigators could see evidence of transport. There was no suspicious temporal puncture that would suggest a hammer blow rather than impact with the platform. The local police quickly filed a report of accidental death, and within hours, the case was closed.
Arun Dey stood on the platform and watched the tracks being cleared. In his pocket, he carried a fresh, blank notebook. Behind him, the ghost of John Lobo's warnings faded into the train whistles.
The system had won its greatest victory. It had not needed to kill the next-generation investigator. It had simply taught him how to build a better locked-room.
And as Arun walked away from the platform, he understood that Lobo's silence—the silence of the exiled investigator—had been a form of resistance compared to what Arun had become. Lobo had chosen not to participate. Arun had chosen to perfect participation.
The machinery continued.
It always continued.
